. Double checked:  G8, connects horizontally to H8, opens vertically. It\'s safe. It gives me potential to move diagonally. And a good triangle. White\'s best move will be to respond to my movement.\n\nLet\'s summarize. So, the play is G8. It gives me a good chance to start a successful advance and to create problems for white. It keeps my pieces from being blocked immediately by their placement. And, G8 connects with my stones H8 and H9 to form a strong triangle. All the boxes are checked.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board shows Black with a vertical block at H8/H9 stopped by White at H10, and limited horizontal potential at row 9 due to White at I9. Black needs to generate new attacking lines using the existing stones. The most promising area is the top-left quadrant relative to the stones, which is open. \n\nMoves like H7 are ineffective because the vertical line is already capped. Moves like G9 or G10 run into immediate horizontal blocks or proximity to White\'s strong formation. \n\nThe move at G8 is the strongest candidate. It connects horizontally with H8 and diagonally with H9. Both resulting lines (G8-H8 and G8-H9) are "live," meaning they are not blocked at the immediate ends and have space to grow into 5. Specifically, G8 forms a stable triangle shape with H8 and H9, offering multiple directions for future attacks (horizontal towards F8, diagonal towards F7/I10). Comparing G8 to I8, G8 is superior because I8 is vertically blocked by White\'s stone at I9, whereas G8 faces open space.\n\nReason: Black\'s primary stones H8 and H9 are blocked vertically and to the right. The move G8 creates a connected triangle shape, establishing two live lines (horizontal and diagonal) simultaneously. It expands into the open area of the board, maximizing potential for creating open 3s and 4s, while avoiding immediate blocks from White\'s stones at H10 and I9.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "G", "row": 8}'}
