ith threats in multiple directions:\n1.  **Vertical:** I9-I10. Playing I8 or I11 creates a "Live 3".\n2.  **Horizontal:** H10-I10. Playing G10 or J10 creates a "Live 3".\n3.  **Diagonal:** H10-I9. Playing J8 or G11 creates a "Live 3".\n\nFor Black, the primary asset is the horizontal pair G8-H8.\n-   If Black plays **F8**, it creates a Live 3 (F8-G8-H8). However, White can respond at **I8**. This move would block Black\'s extension to the right AND create a vertical Live 3 for White (I8-I9-I10). Black would be forced to defend passively, and White would gain a winning initiative.\n-   If Black plays **I8**, it achieves two critical goals:\n    1.  **Attack:** It extends the G8-H8 line to form a **Live 3** (G8-H8-I8), creating an immediate threat that White must answer.\n    2.  **Defense:** It occupies the vital point (I8) that White wants to use for their vertical attack. By taking this spot, Black prevents White from forming the I8-I9-I10 line.\n\nReason:\nI8 is the "Vital Point" for both players. For Black, it creates a powerful Open 3 (Live 3) threat. For White, it is the key to forming a vertical Open 3 while blocking Black. By playing I8, Black seizes the initiative and prevents White from launching a devastating counter-attack. Any other move (like F8) allows White to play I8, putting Black in a losing defensive position.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "I", "row": 8}'}
